From {{inh|en|enm|race}}, partially from {{inh|en|ang|rǣs|t=a race, swift or violent running, rush, onset}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*rās}}; and partially from {{der|en|non|rás|t=a running, race}}; both from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*rēsō|t=a course}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₁reh₁s-|t=to flow, rush}}. Cognate with {{cog|gml|râs}} {{gl|"a strong current"; whence {{cog|nds-de|Raas|t=mad rush, rage, fury}}}}, {{cog|nl|ras|t=a strong whirling current}}, {{cog|da|ræs}}, {{cog|no|-}} and {{cog|sv|ras}}, {{cog|no|rås}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# A [[contest]] [[between]] [[people]], [[animal]]s, [[vehicle]]s, etc. where the [[goal]] is to be the [[first]] to [[reach]] some [[objective]].
#: {{ux|en|Several horses ran in a horse '''race''': the first one to reach the finishing post won.}}
#: {{ux|en|The '''race''' to cure cancer}}
#: {{ux|en|The '''race''' around the [[park]] was won by Johnny, who ran faster than the others.}}
#: {{ux|en|We had a '''race''' to see who could [[finish]] the book the [[quickest]].}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Ecclesiastes|9|11|text=I returned, and saw vnder the Sunne, That the '''race''' is not to the swift, nor the battell to the strong, neither yet bread to the wise, nor yet riches to men of vnderstanding, nor yet fauour to men of skil; but time and chance happeneth to them all.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1743|author=w:Alexander Pope|title=The Dunciad|location=London|publisher=M. Cooper|section=book 2|page=82|lines=58–60|pageurl=https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Dunciad/Book_2#82|text="Behold that rival here! / "The '''race''' by vigour, not by vaunts is won; / "So take the hindmost, Hell."—He said, and run.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2012-11-02|title=After Days of Pressure, Marathon Is Off|journal=The New York Times|author=Ken Belson|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/03/sports/new-york-city-marathon-will-not-be-held-sunday.html |text=After days of intensifying pressure from runners, politicians and the general public to call off the New York City Marathon in the wake of Hurricane Sandy, city officials and the event’s organizers decided Friday afternoon to cancel the '''race'''.}}
# Swift [[progress]]; [[rapid]] [[motion]]; an instance of moving or driving at high speed.
#: {{synonyms|en|dash|running|rush}}
#* {{RQ:Bacon Sylva Sylvarum|edition=3rd|century=VII|chapter=Experiments Solitary touching the Quicknesse of Motion in Birds|page=166|passage=The flight of many birds is swifter than the '''race''' of any beasts.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1805|translator={{w|John Mason Good|Good, John Mason}}|title=The Nature of Things|volume=2|original=w:De rerum natura|by={{w|Lucretius|Titus Lucretius Carus}}|location=London|publisher=Longman, Hurst, Rees, and Orme|lines=190–191|section=book 4|page=33|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/natureofthingsdi02lucr/page/33|text=Hence the rapid '''race''' / Of light, and lustre from th' effusive sun}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1847|month=December|title=The Literature of Humbug|journal=The Young American's Magazine|volume=1|page=318|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=REFAAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA318
|text=And above all, it is an age of activity and enterprise, an age of new discoveries and new deviltries, an age of magnetic telegraphs and Mississippi bonds, and it would be indeed odd if, in the swift '''race''' of progress, the rogue did not keep his natural station in the van of the movement.}}
# {{lb|en|electronics|computing}} A [[race condition]]; a [[bug]] or [[problem]] that occurs when two or more [[component]]s attempt to use the same [[resource]] at the same time.
#: {{synonyms|en|race condition|race hazard}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1989|author=R. Raghuram|title=Computer Simulation of Electronic Circuits|location=New Delhi|publisher=Wiley Eastern|isbn=9788122401110|page=181|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=nVaCwXp_S8wC&pg=PA79|text=Many problems of oscillations and '''races''' are solved by this arrangement.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1999|author=Max Hailperin; Barbara Kaiser; Karl Knight|chapter=Java, Applets, and Concurrency|title=Concrete Abstractions|publisher=Brooks/Cole Publishing|isbn=9780534952112|page=622|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=yYyVRueWlZ8C&pg=PA622|text=Because a '''race''' by definition depends on the timing being just wrong, you could test your program any number of times, never observe any misbehavior, and still have a user run into the problem.¶ This occurrence is not just a theoretical possibility: Real programs have race bugs and real users have encountered them, sometimes with consequences that have literally been fatal.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Charles P. Pfleeger; Shari Lawrence Pfleeger|title=Analyzing Computer Security|publisher=Prentice Hall|page=79|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=nVaCwXp_S8wC&pg=PA79|text=As the name implies, a race condition means that two processes are competing within the same time interval, and the '''race''' affects the integrity or correctness of the computing tasks.}}
# A [[sequence]] of [[event]]s; a [[progressive]] [[movement]] toward a goal.
#: {{synonyms|en|course|procedure|process|train|Thesaurus:sequence}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1603|author=w:Ben Jonson|title=Sejanus His Fall|url=https://www.gutenberg.org/files/5232/5232-h/5232-h.htm|section=act 2, scene 2|text=A '''race''' of wicked acts / Shall flow out of my anger, and o’erspread / The world’s wide face[.]}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1624|author=Francis Bacon|editor=Basil Montagu|chapter=Considerations Touching a War with Spain|title=The Works of Francis Bacon|volume=5|publisher=William Pickering
|year_published=1826|page=240|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/worksfrancisbac12conggoog/page/240|text=An offensive war is made, which is unjust in the aggressor; the prosecution and '''race''' of the war carrieth the defendant to invade the ancient patrimony of the first aggressor, who is now turned defendant; shall he sit down, and not put himself in defence?}}
# A [[fast-moving]] [[current]] of water.
#: {{synonyms|en|rip}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1893|chapter=Remarks upon the Way from Abingdon to Southamption, and other Places|title=The Manuscripts of His Grace the Duke of Portland|volume=2|location=London|publisher=Her Majesty's Stationery Office|page=288|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=1asKAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA288|text=Here are in these seas two dangerous '''races''', the one called St. Alban's, the other Portland Race.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1980|author=Pauline H. Gurewitz|title=Hydraulic Research in the United States and Canada, 1978|page=120|text=The existing analysis and program for the propeller-rudder interaction has been updated incorporating all the improvements concerned with the propeller loading distribution, including that associated with the fact that the rudder is immersed in the '''race''' of the propeller.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2003|month=December|author=Jonathan Raban|title=Julia and the Whirlpools|journal=Cruising World|volume=29|number=12|page=40|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=B4AeuY4wnB0C&pg=PA40|text=This is an area of spectacular tidal '''races''', rips, swirls, boils, whirlpools, overfalls, currents, and countercurrents. Scylla and Charybdis pale by comparison with the great maelstroms where the sea is trapped between Vancouver Island and the Canadian mainland.}}
# A water [[channel]], especially one built to lead water to or from a point where it is utilised, such as that which [[powers]] a [[millwheel]].
#: {{hyponyms|en|headrace|mill race|wheel-race|tailrace}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1885|author=w:James Leal Greenleaf|title=Reports on the Water-Power of the United States|section=part 1|chapter=Report on the Water-Powers of the Drainage Basins of Lakes Huron and Erie, in the United States|location=Washington|publisher=Department of the Interior|pages=504–505|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=nyM-AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA502-IA7|text=Evidently the future manufacturing development depends upon the hydraulic canal, so far as existing works are concerned, rather than upon the two '''races''', which can never be enlarged to embrace a comprehensive improvement of the river, while the capabilities at th hydraulic basin are unrivaled.  So far as can be learned there is no expectation of ever increasing materially the capacity of the '''races'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1888|chapter=Water Rights|title=Gold Mining Regulations, 1888|publisher=Parliament of South Australia|page=4|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=D0FNAQAAIAAJ&pg=RA10-PA4|section=48|text=Any miners intending to divert and use water for mining or general purposes, or to cut a '''race''' or construct dams or reservoirs in connection therewith, shall give notice in writing thereof to the Warden {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=1957-12-16|author=A. H. Mouat; R. C. Stuart; G. Mason|title=Farming in Ida Valley, Central Otago|journal=The New Zealand Journal of Agriculture|volume=95|number=6|page=587|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=VrnxRUx9kuUC&pg=RA1-PA587|text=Water for irrigation is stored in the high country behind the Upper Manorburn Dam.  Two parallel '''races''' at different levels run along the west side of the valley and one '''race''' flowing along the east side is supplemented by water stored at the Poolburn Dam.}}
# A [[path]] that something or someone [[move]]s [[along]].
#: {{synonyms|en|career|course|progress}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Regained|book=Samson Agonistes|40|line=598|passage=My '''race''' of glory run, and '''race''' of ſhame,}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1852|author=w:Harriet Beecher Stowe|title=w:Uncle Tom's Cabin|location=Boston|publisher=John P. Jewett & Company|volume=2|page=242|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/uncletomscabinor02instow/page/242/|text=There were all the marked passages, which had thrilled his soul so often,—words of patriarchs and seers, poets and sages, who from early time had spoken courage to man,—voices from the great cloud of witnesses who ever surround us in the '''race''' of life.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=Chad Taylor|title=The Cry of the Harvest|page=115|text=Don't let fear be a factor for you as the finish line of harvest calls out to you to join the '''race''' of eternity. Clear the table of excuses and go!}}
# A [[guide]] or channel that a [[component]] of a [[machine]] moves along:
## {{lb|en|sewing|weaving}} A [[groove]] on a [[sewing machine]] or a [[loom]] along which the [[shuttle]] moves.
##: {{synonyms|en|shuttle race}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1860|author=Charles Cole|title=The Sewing Machine, and its Capabilities|page=53|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=PiVcAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA53|text=I have lately seen a shuttle machine of Messrs. Grover Baker's construction, in which the shuttle worked in a semi-circular '''race''' and produced two stitches at each revolution of the wheel.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=1872-11-29|title=Improved Loom for Weaving Fabrics of Any Width|journal=The English Mechanic and Word of Science|volume=16|number=401|page=259|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=LrXmAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA259|text=Meanwhile another lug on the shuttle-band engages another carrier at the other end of the loom, and the belt, continuing to move in the same direction, conveys the carrier across the '''race''' in a similar manner as above described.}}
## {{lb|en|engineering}} A [[ring]] with a groove in which rolling elements (such as balls) ride, forming part of a rolling-element [[bearing]] (for example, a [[ball bearing]]).
##* {{quote-book|en|date=1965-08-15|title=Maintenance of Aeronautical Antifriction Bearings|others=NAVWEPS 01-1A-503|publisher=United States Bureau of Naval Weapons|section=2|page=5|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=M861fCPQGssC&pg=PA5|text=These bearings do not employ a loading groove or filling slot but utilize an uninterrupted '''race''' groove containing the maximum number of balls that can be introduced by eccentric displacement of the '''races'''.  Due to the relatively large size of the balls and the fact that the ball curvature is only slightly less than the '''race''' curvature, the bearings have comparatively high load carrying capacity in both axial and radial directions.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1999|author=Steve Goldman|title=Vibration Spectrum Analysis|edition=2nd|publisher=Industrial Press|location=New York|isbn=9780831130886|page=90|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=DtvWhuKHGvwC&pg=PA90|text=The chances of picking up an inner '''race''' fault are small unless the load direction of the bearing coincides with the location of the accelerometer.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2017|author=Tian Ran Lin; Kun Yu; Jiwen Tan|chapter=Condition Monitoring and Fault Diagnosis of Roller Element Bearing|doi=10.5772/67143|title=Bearing Technology|editor=Pranav H. Darji|publisher=InTech|location=Rijeka, Croatia|isbn=9789535131830|page=40|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=zvSODwAAQBAJ&pg=PA40|text=The bearing comprises four mechanical components: an outer '''race''', an inner '''race''', rollers (balls), and a cage that holds the rollers (balls) in place.}}
# {{lb|en|gambling}} A [[keno]] [[gambling]] [[session]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2022|author=Kevin Blackwood; Swain Scheps|chapter=Striking the Mother Lode: Keno and Bingo|title=Casino Gambling For Dummies|edition=2nd|publisher=John Wiley & Sons|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=sl1lEAAAQBAJ&pg=PT293|text=Your odds are sometimes significantly better with video keno {{...}} But because video keno plays so much faster, you're likely to lose more money over a given period. Live keno '''races''' start every 10 minutes, but you can make 100 bets on a video version in the same amount of time.}}

