##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1881|month=July|author=w:Edward Burnett Tylor|journal=Popular Science Monthly|title=The '''Races''' of Mankind|url=https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Popular_Science_Monthly/Volume_19/July_1881/The_Races_of_Mankind|volume=19|page=309|text=The '''race''' to which most anthropologists refer the native Americans is the Mongoloid of Eastern Asia, who are capable of accommodating themselves to the extremest climates, and who by the form of skull, the light brown skin, straight black hair, and black eyes, show considerable agreement with the American tribes.}}
##* {{quote-av|en|year=1958|writers={{w|Alan Burgess|Burgess, Alan}}; {{w|Isobel Lennart|Lennart, Isobel}}|title=w:The Inn of the Sixth Happiness|url=https://archive.org/details/the-inn-of-the-sixth-happiness|oclc=732947584|time=1:41:15|publisher=20th Century Fox|roles={{w|Curd Jürgens}} as Colonel Lin Nan and {{w|Ingrid Bergman}} as Gladys Aylward|text=Colonel Lin Nan: Would it offend you to be loved by a man of another '''race'''?<br />Gladys Aylward: It would honor me.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=2012|month=March-April|author=w:Jan Sapp|title=Race Finished|volume=100|issue=2|page=164|magazine=w:American Scientist|url=http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/race-finished|passage=Few concepts are as emotionally charged as that of '''race'''. The word conjures up a mixture of associations—culture, ethnicity, genetics, subjugation, exclusion and persecution. But is the tragic history of efforts to define groups of people by '''race''' really a matter of the misuse of science, the abuse of a valid biological concept?}}
