##* {{quote-book|en|year=1968|month=December|author=Dale W. Rice; Victor B. Scheffer|title=A List of the Marine Mammals of the World|others=Special Scientific Report—Fisheries number 579|section=Phoca vitulina|publisher=United States Fish and Wildlife Service|page=6|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=h-PYLeB1h5wC&pg=RA4-PA4|text=Two '''races''' are certainly valid.  The Atlantic '''race''' (P. v. vitulina) is distinguishable from the Pacific '''race''' (P. v. richardi Gray, 1864) by skull characters.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2000|author=[[w:E. O. Wilson|Edward O[sborne] Wilson]]|title=Sociobiology: The New Synthesis|edition=25th anniversary|publisher=Harvard University Press|isbn=9780674002357|page=9|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=FEsCBAAAQBAJ&pg=PA9|text=A population that differs signicatly from other populations belonging to the same species is referred to as a ''geographic '''race''''' or ''subspecies''.  Subspecies are separated from other subspecies by distance and geographic barriers that prevent the exchange of individuals, as opposed to the genetically based "intrinsic isolating mechanisms" that hold species apart.}}
## {{lb|en|botany}} A strain of [[plant]] with characteristics causing it to differ from other plants of the same [[species]].
##: {{hyponyms|en|cultigen|cultivar|indigen}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1859 |author=w:Charles Darwin |chapter=Variation under Domestication|title=w:On the Origin of Species|passage=Nevertheless, as our varieties certainly do occasionally revert in some of their characters to ancestral forms, it seems to me not improbable, that if we could succeed in naturalising, or were to cultivate, during many generations, the several '''races''', for instance, of the cabbage, in very poor soil (in which case, however, some effect would have to be attributed to the direct action of the poor soil), that they would to a large extent, or even wholly, revert to the wild aboriginal stock.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1948|month=June|chapter=Development of '''Races'''|title=Woody-Plant Seed Manual|others=Miscellaneous Publication no. 654|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=United States Department of Agriculture|page=15|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=jfnQ3hVDpmgC&pg=PA15|text=Tree '''races''' develop not only in different latitudes, but also at different altitudes and within mountainous regions.  Since climate changes markedly with altitude as well as latitude, both kinds of development are included in the term climatic '''races'''.  In addition, soil or site '''races''' may develop in areas similar climatically but characterized by different soil or site conditions.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|start_date=11 September 1995|date=14 September 1995|title=A Genetic Evaluation of Three Potential '''Races''' of the Rare Kachina Daisy|author=Loreen Allphin; Michael D[ennis] Windham; Kimball T[aylor] Harper|journal=Southwestern Rare and Endangered Plants: Proceedings of the Second Conference|location=Flagstaff, Arizona|page=75|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=OvRzhwAPjnoC&pg=PA75|text=Our genetic evaluation suggests that the morphologically distinct '''race''' (Dolores River) is more closely related to the type materials than the ecologically distinct, high-elevation '''race'''.}}
## {{lb|en|animal husbandry}} A [[breed]] or [[strain]] of [[domesticate]]d animal.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=c. 1596–1599|author=w:William Shakespeare|title=w:The Merchant of Venice|section=act 5, scene 1|passage=For do but note a wild and wanton herd, / Or '''race''' of youthful and unhandled colts, / Fetching mad bounds.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1799|author=Joshua Rowlin|title=The Complete Cow-Doctor; Or, Farmer's Companion|location=London|edition=2nd|page=42|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=vHdZAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA42|text=They have another breed, called the Dunlop cows, which are allowed to be the best '''race''' for yielding milk in Great Britain or Ireland, not only for large quantities, but also for richness in quality.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1875|title=The Living World|author=Augustus C. L. Arnold|volume=1|page=88|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=jasaAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA88|location=Boston|publisher=Samuel Walker & Co|text=Great St. Bernard Dog—This '''race''' is nearly allied to the Newfoundland Dog in form, stature, hair, and colors; but the head and ears are like that of a Water Spaniel.}}
## {{lb|en|mycology|bacteriology}} A [[strain]] of [[microorganism]], [[fungi]], etc.
##: {{synonyms|en|pathotype|pathovar}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=1977-03-24|title=Why is cereal fungus so resistant?|journal=New Scientist|volume=73|number=1044|page=697|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=fqCC-Lcdn3oC&pg=PA697|text=Now Mary MacDonald of the Plant Breeding Institute at Maris Lane, Cambridge, has made an interesting study which has duplicated the conditions under which new '''races''' arise. And she has produced at least one new fungal '''race'''.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=2018|month=December|author=Anna Kolobaeva; Olga Kotik|title=Technological Approaches to Cider Quality|journal=Advances in Engineering Research|volume=151|publisher=Atlantis Press|doi=10.2991/agrosmart-18.2018.68|url=https://www.atlantis-press.com/article/55908713.pdf|text=The type of microorganisms is a very important factor influencing the quality of cider. Yeast of various producers and '''races''' result in different taste and flavor.}}
