====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# To [[assign]] a race to; to perceive as having a (usually specified) race.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1996|title=Philosophical Studies in Education|page=151|passage=To be '''raced''' as black in the U.S. translates symbolically into being considered inferior to whites, lazy, immoral, boisterous, violent, and sexually promiscuous.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=Athena D. Mutua|title=Progressive Black Masculinities?|publisher=Routledge|isbn=9781135869274|page=30|passage=From this perspective, the project of progressive blackness entails the edification of black people and the elimination of all forms of domination that limit this edification for all those '''raced''' as black.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=George Yancy|title=Black Bodies, White Gazes: The Continuing Significance of Race|publisher=Rowman & Littlefield|isbn=9780742552982|page=46|passage=By avoiding being '''raced''' as white, whites are able to maintain the illusion that they have always been individuals, that they have always accomplished their achievements through merit alone.}}
#* <!--to show use without an "as" clause, and in another verb tense-->{{quote-web|en|date=2020-03-24|author=Sophie Lewis|url=https://www.opendemocracy.net/en/oureconomy/coronavirus-crisis-shows-its-time-abolish-family|site=opendemocracy.net|title=The coronavirus crisis shows it's time to abolish the family|text=[T]he private family qua mode of social reproduction still, frankly, sucks. It genders, nationalizes and '''races''' us. It norms us for productive work.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To pass down certain [[phenotypic]] traits to [[offspring]].
#: {{synonyms|en|come true|breed true}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1738|origyear=1728|author=w:Ephraim Chambers|chapter=Race|title=Cyclopaedia: Or, An Universal Dictionary Of Arts and Sciences|location=London|publisher=D. Midwinter|edition=2nd|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=DHOj9vGhuroC&pg=PP496|text=D'Hervieux obſerves that it is uſual to put the female canary bird to the male goldfinch, linnet, or the like, to breed; but for his part, he ſhould chuſe to put the male canary-bird to the female goldfinch, linnet, &c. becauſe the male uſually '''races''' more than the female, ''i. e.'' the young ones take more after the male than after the female.}}

===Etymology 3===
[[File:Ingwer 2 (fcm).jpg|thumb|230px|A race of ginger.]]

Mid 16th century. From {{der|en|frm|raïz}}, {{m|frm|raiz}}, {{m|frm|rais||root}}, from {{der|en|la|radix||root}}, from {{der|en|itc-pro|*wrādīks}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*wréh₂ds}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[rhizome]] or [[root]], ''especially'' of [[ginger]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1610|author=w:William Shakespeare|title=w:The Winter's Tale|section=act IV, scene III|line=45
|passage=I must have saffron to color the warden pies; mace; dates, none—that's out of my note; nutmegs, seven; a '''race''' or two of ginger, but that I may beg; four pounds of prunes, and as many of raisins o' th' sun.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1777|author=[[w:Edward Terry (author)|Edward Terry]]|title=A Voyage to East-India|page=62|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=nnUIAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA92|text=They have onions and garlick, and some herbs and small roots for sallads; and in the southernmoft parts, ginger growing almost in every place; the large '''races''' whereof are there very excellently well preserved, as we may know by our tasting them in England.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1842|author=Gibbons Merle|title=The Domestic Dictionary and Housekeeper's Manual| page=433| passage=On the third day after this second boiling, pour all the syrup into a pan, put the '''races''' of ginger with it, and boil it up until the syrup adheres to the spoon.}}

